"mp_arc 21-37 (https://web.ma.utexas.edu/mp_arc-bin/mpa?yn=21-37)\nciteturn10search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago;     Random Regular Bipartite Graphs Satisfy Weak Virial Positivity, for a Large Range of the Parameters (13K, LaTeX) Jul 9, 21 ... We now more particularly define the ``Virial Positivity Conjecture\" as the conjecture that the fraction of graphs that satisfy Delta^k u(i) go to 0 for all k > 1 and i, approaches 1 as n goes to infinity.\n21-37 Paul Federbush\n    Random Regular Bipartite Graphs Satisfy Weak Virial Positivity, for a Large Range of the Parameters (13K, LaTeX) Jul 9, 21\n    Abstract , Paper (src), View paper (auto. generated ps), Index of related papers\n\n    Abstract. We deal with r-regular bipartite graphs with 2n vertices. In a previous paper, Butera, Pernici and the author have introduced a quantity u(i), u(i) = -ln(i!m_i), a function of the number of i-matchings, m_i, and conjectured that the fraction of graphs that violate Delta^k u(i) > 0 for k > 1 vanishes as n goes to infinity. Here Delta is the finite difference operator. We now more particularly define the ``Virial Positivity Conjecture\" as the conjecture that the fraction of graphs that satisfy Delta^k u(i) go to 0 for all k > 1 and i, approaches 1 as n goes to infinity. The ``Weak Virial Positivity Conjecture\" is the conjecture that for each i and k > 1 the probability that Delta^k u(i) > 0 goes to 1 as n goes to infinity. The term Virial is used since the condition Delta^k u(i) > 0 corresponds to the positivity of the Virial coefficients for infinite regular lattices. Herein we prove Weak Virial Positivity for the range of parameters r < 11, i+k < 101, 1 < k < 28. A formalism of Wanless as systematized by Pernici is central to this effort. Basically this paper is a corollary to our parallel attack on graph positivity in a previous paper. We assume basic knowledge of this previous paper\n\n    Files: 21-37.src( 21-37.comments , 21-37.keywords , june.tex )--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSquare-free reducts of words (https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.12822)\nciteturn10academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.9 years ago; Title: Square-free reducts of words ... Moreover, the function counting the maximum number of reducts a ternary word of length $n$ may have grows exponentially. ... A few open questions are posed concerning some structures on words defined with the use of the square reduction.\nTitle: Square-free reducts of words\nAuthors: Jarosław Grytczuk, Szymon Stankiewicz\nDate: Wed Nov 25 15:26:33 2020\n\nA \\emph{square} is a finite non-empty word consisting of two identical adjacent blocks. A word is \\emph{square-free} if it does not contain a square as a factor. In any finite word one may delete the repeated block of a square, obtaining thereby a shorter word. By repeating this process, a square-free word is eventually reached, which we call a \\emph{reduct} of the original word. How many different reducts a single word may have? It is not hard to prove that any binary word has exactly one reduct. We prove that there exist ternary words with arbitrarily many reducts. Moreover, the function counting the maximum number of reducts a ternary word of length $n$ may have grows exponentially. We also prove that over four letters, there exist words with any given number of reducts, which does not seem to be the case for ternary words. Finally, we demonstrate that the set of all finite ternary words splits into finitely many classes of \\emph{related} words (one may get from one word to the other by a sequence of square reductions and factor duplications). A few open questions are posed concerning some structures on words defined with the use of the square reduction.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNumerical Semigroups of small and large type (https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.08110)\nciteturn10academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Title: Numerical Semigroups of small and large type ... Some of the key properties of a numerical semigroup are its Frobenius number F, genus g and type t.\nTitle: Numerical Semigroups of small and large type\nAuthors: Deepesh Singhal\nDate: Tue Aug 18 18:14:36 2020\n\nA numerical semigroup is a sub-semigroup of the natural numbers that has a finite complement. Some of the key properties of a numerical semigroup are its Frobenius number F, genus g and type t. It is known that for any numerical semigroup $\\frac{g}{F+1-g}\\leq t\\leq 2g-F$. Numerical semigroups with $t=2g-F$ are called almost symmetric, we introduce a new property that characterises them. We give an explicit characterisation of numerical semigroups with $t=\\frac{g}{F+1-g}$. We show that for a fixed $\\alpha$ the number of numerical semigroups with Frobenius number $F$ and type $F-\\alpha$ is eventually constant for large $F$. Also the number of numerical semigroups with genus $g$ and type $g-\\alpha$ is also eventually constant for large $g$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite-component conjecture for square-reduction graphs — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/348789/finite-component-conjecture-for-square-reduction-graphs)\nciteturn10search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Conjecture open Open about 6 years · documented by 2020\n\nFor each $k\\geqslant 2$k⩾2, let $\\mathcal{G}_k$G k​ be the graph whose vertices are square-free words over a $k$k-letter alphabet, with edges arising from the square-reduction relation. ... source: Jarosław Grytczuk and Szymon Stankiewicz, “Square-free reducts of words”, arXiv:2011.12822 (2020).\n\ncombinatorics-on-words graph-theory word-reductions\n\nFinite-component conjecture for square-reduction graphs\n\nConjecture openOpen about 6 years · documented by 2020\n\nFor each $k\\geqslant 2$k⩾2, let $\\mathcal{G}_k$G k​ be the graph whose vertices are square-free words over a $k$k-letter alphabet, with edges arising from the square-reduction relation. Finite-components conjecture. For every $k\\geqslant 2$k⩾2, the graph $\\mathcal{G}_k$G k​ has finitely many connected components. The paper proves this for $k=3$k=3; whether the analogous statement holds for every alphabet size $k\\geqslant 2$k⩾2 remains open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Jarosław Grytczuk and Szymon Stankiewicz, “Square-free reducts of words”, arXiv:2011.12822 (2020).\n\ncombinatorics-on-wordsgraph-theoryword-reductions\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 3 days ago\n\n\n## 0 Replies\n\n* * *\n\nSign in to reply.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Numerical Semigroups of small and large type (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/343759057_Numerical_Semigroups_of_small_and_large_type)\nciteturn10search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; NUMERICAL SEMIGROUPS OF SMALL AND LARGE TYPE 3 ... NUMERICAL SEMIGROUPS OF SMALL AND LARGE TYPE 9 ... Further we conjecture the following about the growth of T(F, α).\n#{S|F(S) = F, t(S)≡F(mod 2)}>#{S|F(S) = F, t(S)6≡ F(mod 2)}.\n\nConjecture 7.2.\n\nlim\n\nF→∞\n\n#{S|F(S) = F, t(S)≡F(mod 2)}\n\n#{S|F(S) = F}=1\n\n2.\n\nNUMERICAL SEMIGROUPS OF SMALL AND LARGE TYPE 17\n\nThe data in Table 1 has been used to plot the ratio of numerical\n\nsemigroups in which t(S)≡F(mod 2) among all numerical semigroups\n\nwith Frobenius number F.\n\nF Odd t Even t F Odd t Even t F Odd t Even t\n\n2 0 1 12 11 29 22 857 1056\n\n3 2 0 13 68 38 23 2320 1776\n\n4 0 2 14 41 62 24 1524 2054\n\n5 4 1 15 131 69 25 4573 3700\n\n6 1 3 16 73 132 26 3779 4396\n\n7 8 3 17 283 182 27 8803 7329\n\n8 2 8 18 165 240 28 7547 8720\n\n9 15 6 19 571 390 29 18656 16247\n\n10 8 14 20 368 532 30 15023 16799\n\n11 35 16 21 1073 755\n\nTable 1. Number of numerical semigroups with odd,\n\neven type. Counted by Frobenius number.\n\nFurther we conjecture the following about the growth of T(F, α).\n\nConjecture 7.3. For any α≥2 and r∈ {0,1}the following limits\n\nexist\n\nlim\n\nF≡r(mod 2),F →∞\n\nlog 2(T(F, α))\n\nF.\n\nWhen r= 0 we denote the conjectured limit by a α and when r= 1\n\nwe denote it by b α. Corollary 6.3.1 implies that a α, b α are at least 0.1.\n\nMoreover Corollary 6.2.1 implies that a α for even α and b α for odd α\n\nare at least 1\n\n6.\n\nConjecture 7.4. The two sequences a α and b α are both strictly in-\n\ncreasing sequences.\n\n18 DEEPESH SINGHAL\n\nWe plot log 2(T(F,α))\n\nF for even and odd F and α= 2,3,4,5. The limits\n\ndo appear to exist.\n\nSimilar patterns are observed when we count by genus. However\n\nthis time numerical semigroups with odd type seem to outnumber the\n\nnumerical semigroups with even type. Table 2 veriﬁes Conjecture 7.5\n\nfor g≤20.\n\nConjecture 7.5. For every g we have\n\n#{S|g(S) = g, t(S)≡1(mod 2)} ≥ #{S|g(S) = g, t(S)≡0(mod 2)}.\n\nConjecture 7.6.\n\nlim\n\ng→∞\n\n#{S|g(S) = g, t(S)≡1(mod 2)}\n\n#{S|g(S) = g}=1\n\n2\n\nWe plot the ratio of numerical semigroups with odd type among\n\nnumerical semigroups with genus g based on Table 2.\n\nNUMERICAL SEMIGROUPS OF SMALL AND LARGE TYPE 19\n\ng Odd t Even t g Odd t Even t g Odd t Even t\n\n1 1 0 8 35 32 15 1555 1302\n\n2 1 1 9 68 50 16 2548 2258\n\n3 3 1 10 116 88 17 4307 3738\n\n4 4 3 11 191 152 18 7060 6407\n\n5 7 5 12 321 271 19 11804 10660\n\n6 13 10 13 552 449 20 19464 17932\n\n7 24 15 14 908 785\n\nTable 2. Number of numerical semigroups with odd,\n\neven type. Counted by genus.\n\nConjecture 7.7. For any α the following limits exist\n\nlim\n\ng→∞\n\nlog φ(L(g, α))\n\ng\n\nHere φ is the golden ratio, we denote the limit by c α.\n\nConjecture 7.8. The sequence c α is monotonically increasing.\n\nWe plot log φ(L(g,α))\n\ng for α= 1,2,3,4,5. The limits do appear to exist.\n\n20 DEEPESH SINGHAL\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Ojeda, I; Garc´ıa-S´anchez, P.A. (2020). Almost symmetric numerical semigroups\n\nwith high type. Turkish Journal of Mathematics. 43. 2499 – 2510. 10.3906/mat-\n\n1906-10.\n\n[2] Froberg, R.; Gottlieb, C.; H¨aggkvist, R. (1986). On numerical semigroups. Semi-\n\ngroup Forum. 35. 63-83. 10.1007/BF02573091.\n\n[3] Backelin, J. (1990). On the number of semigroups of natural numbers. Mathe-\n\nmatica Scandinavica, 66, 197-215. https://doi.org/10.7146/math.scand.a-12304\n\n[4] Rosales J.C.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRandom Regular Bipartite Graphs Satisfy Weak Virial Positivity, for a Large Range of the Parameters (https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05110)\nciteturn10academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Herein we prove Weak Virial Positivity for the range of parameters $r < 11$, $i+k < 101$, $1 < k < 28$,or $i+k < 30$ all $r$.\nTitle: Random Regular Bipartite Graphs Satisfy Weak Virial Positivity, for a Large Range of the Parameters\nAuthors: Paul Federbush\nDate: Sun Jul 11 18:49:40 2021\n\nWe deal with $r$-regular bipartite graphs with $2n$ vertices. In a previous paper, Butera, Pernici and the author have introduced a quantity $u(i)$, $u(i) = -\\ln(i!m\\_i)$, a function of the number of $i$-matchings, $m\\_i$, and conjectured that the fraction of graphs that violate $\\Delta^k u(i) > 0$ for $k > 1$ vanishes as $n$ goes to infinity. Here $\\Delta$ is the finite difference operator. We now more particularly define the \"Virial Positivity Conjecture\" as the conjecture that the fraction of graphs that satisfy $\\Delta^k u(i)$ go to 0 for all $k > 1$ and $i$, approaches 1 as $n$ goes to infinity. The \"Weak Virial Positivity Conjecture\" is the conjecture that for each $i$ and $k > 1$ the probability that $\\Delta^k u(i) > 0$ goes to $1$ as $n$ goes to infinity. The term Virial is used since the condition $\\Delta^k u(i) > 0$ corresponds to the positivity of the Virial coefficients for infinite regular lattices. Herein we prove Weak Virial Positivity for the range of parameters $r < 11$, $i+k < 101$, $1 < k < 28$,or $i+k < 30$ all $r$. A formalism of Wanless as systematized by Pernici is central to this effort. Basically this paper is a corollary to our parallel attack on graph positivity in a previous paper. We assume basic knowledge of this previous paper.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNumerical Semigroups of small and large type - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2008.08110v1)\nciteturn10search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Numerical Semigroups of small and large type ... Some of the key properties of a numerical semigroup are its Frobenius number F, genus g and type t.\n\n## Numerical Semigroups of small and large type\n\nDeepesh Singhal\n\nPublished: 2020-08-18\n\nComments: 20 pages\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1142/s0218196721500417\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nA numerical semigroup is a sub-semigroup of the natural numbers that has a finite complement. Some of the key properties of a numerical semigroup are its Frobenius number F, genus g and type t. It is known that for any numerical semigroup $\\frac{g}{F+1-g}\\leq t\\leq 2g-F$. Numerical semigroups with $t=2g-F$ are called almost symmetric, we introduce a new property that characterises them. We give an explicit characterisation of numerical semigroups with $t=\\frac{g}{F+1-g}$. We show that for a fixed $α$ the number of numerical semigroups with Frobenius number $F$ and type $F-α$ is eventually constant for large $F$. Also the number of numerical semigroups with genus $g$ and type $g-α$ is also eventually constant for large $g$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe generalized Delta conjecture at t=0 (https://anna.vanden.wyngaerd.web.ulb.be/pdfs/The%20generalized%20Delta%20conjecture%20at%20t%3D0.pdf)\nciteturn10search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; For example, consider the partially labelled Dyck path of Figure 2: its dinv reading word is 23154, while its pmaj reading word is 23145. ... Given an ordered multiset partition π, we define its dinv (resp. maj) reading word as the dinv (resp. pmaj) reading word of the corresponding partially labelled Dyck path ξ^{-1}(π) (possibly allowing a blank in the bottom left corner), where ξ is the obvious extension of the map in Proposition 4.12.\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 3: A partially labelled decorated Dyck path with dinv 0. The diagram shows a grid with a blue Dyck path boundary, a faint diagonal from bottom-left to top-right, and circled labels on the left side of the path reading 3, 1, 0, 6, 0, 5, 4, 2 from top to bottom, with asterisks marking some rows.\n</visual_element>\n\nExample 4.15. For example, consider the partially labelled Dyck path of Figure 2: its dinv reading word is 23154, while its pmaj reading word is 23145.\n\nDefinition 4.16. We define the set SOP(m,n)^k of standardized ordered multiset partitions to be the set OP(m,(1,1,...,1))^k ⊂ OP(m,n)^k of all ordered multiset partitions of type (m,(1,1,...,1)) with m + k blocks. We define SOP^L(m,n)^k and SOP^R(m,n)^k as the subsets of SOP(m,n)^k of the partitions that do not contain a 0 in their leftmost or rightmost block, respectively.\n\nGiven an ordered multiset partition π, we define its dinv (resp. maj) reading word as the dinv (resp. pmaj) reading word of the corresponding partially labelled Dyck path ξ^{-1}(π) (possibly allowing a blank in the bottom left corner), where ξ is the obvious extension of the map in Proposition 4.12. We define its inv reading word as its dinv reading word.\n\nDefinition 4.17. Given an ordered multiset partition π ∈ OP(m,n) we can define the standardization with respect to a statistic (it being inv, dinv, or maj) as the unique standard multiset partition obtained from π by replacing its 1’s by 1,2,...,α₁, its 2’s by α₁+1,...,α₁+α₂ and so on, in such a way that the reverse reading word (with respect to the relevant statistic, cf. Definition 4.14) of the standardization is an α-shuffle (i.e. it is in 1,...,α₁ ⊔ α₁+1,...,α₁+α₂ ⊔ ...).\n\nExample 4.18. Consider the ordered multiset partition 10 | 2 | 410 | 21 ∈ OP^R_2((3,2,0,1))^2. Its standardization with respect to both dinv and inv is 10 | 5 | 620 | 43, whose reverse dinv (or inv) reading word is 612453 = 1,2,3 ⊔ 4,5 ⊔ 6, while its standardization with respect to maj is 10 | 4 | 620 | 53, whose reverse pmaj reading word is 146253 = 1,2,3 ⊔ 4,5 ⊔ 6.\n\nThe verification of the following proposition is straightforward, hence left to the reader.\n\nProposition 4.19. The standardization with respect to one of the statistics dinv, inv or maj preserves that statistic.\n\nUsing the well-known theory of shuffles (see e.g. [11, Chapter 6]), and the previous proposition, it is clear that\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\pi \\in OP^L_{(m,n)^k}} q^{\\mathrm{dinv}(\\pi)} x^\\pi\n=\n\\sum_{\\pi \\in SOP^L_{(m,n)^k}} q^{\\mathrm{dinv}(\\pi)} Q_{\\mathrm{ides}(\\sigma(\\pi))},\n\\]\n\nwhere σ(π) denotes the dinv reading word of π and ides(σ) is the descent set of σ^{-1}.\n\n16--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeepesh Singhal (https://sites.uci.edu/singhald/)\nciteturn10search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Contemporary Mathematics, The Ideal Theory and Arithmetic of Rings, Monoids, and Semigroups. https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/836 ... Numerical Semigroups of small and large type. ... On the asymptotic growth of the number of associated numerical sets (with A.\n  9. The Expected Embedding Dimension, type and weight of a Numerical Semigroup (with N. Kaplan). 2023. Enumerative Combinatorics and Applications. 3:2, #S2R14 https://doi.org/10.54550/ECA2023V3S2R14\n  10. Frobenius allowable gaps of Generalized Numerical Semigroups (with Y. Lin). 2022. Electronic Journal of Combinatorics. Volume 29, Issue 4. https://doi.org/10.37236/10748